  "summary": "I found no concrete defects in this fix for finding I-2.\n\nThe new order is right. In `apps/desktop/src/main.ts:249-255`, the Clerk layer is built first, and it does not yield until the bridge exists. Its `make` now registers `open-url` and `open-file` right after the bridge, at `apps/desktop/src/app/DesktopClerk.ts:129-150`. `ElectronApp.on` is a synchronous `acquireRelease` around `Electron.app.on`, so registering adds no point where startup can yield. That puts the listeners ahead of `shellEnvironment.installIntoProcess`, `desktopSettings.load` and `clerk.configure` (`DesktopApp.ts:283, 309, 323`). The existing `runSync` layer-build test, now with a packaged macOS case, would catch it if the layer ever started suspending.\n\nI checked these paths and they behave correctly:\n\n- **Draining the queue:** at `DesktopClerk.ts:216-222`, both handlers are swapped and the queue is drained inside one `Effect.sync`. JS is single-threaded, so an event can't arrive mid-swap and nothing is delivered twice. Drained links go through the existing `webLinks.receive`, which still holds them until the renderer says it is ready.\n- **Provider auth URLs:** the Codex handoff (`t3code://auth/codex`) and `resumeProviderAuth` only accept custom-scheme URLs. So no auth URL can be taken for an http(s) web link and queued during the early window.\n- **Secondary instance:** the listeners are only registered when `bridge.isPrimaryInstance` is true, which keeps the \"registers nothing\" test valid. `configure` still quits and interrupts as before.\n- **No duplicates:** the old registrations inside `configure` were removed, so each event has one listener. `second-instance` stays in `configure`, and the order the existing test checks (`open-url`, `open-file`, `second-instance`) still holds.\n- **Cleanup:** the listeners now live for the layer's lifetime, which is the whole runtime, and are removed on release. The new test confirms both listener counts go back to zero.\n- **Unrelated events:** `mailto:` links and `.txt` files are neither claimed with `preventDefault` nor queued, and no queued page is sent again.\n\nOne thing is unchanged but worth knowing. A provider-auth `t3code://` link that arrives in the same early window is still dropped, because `openProviderAuth` returns false until `configure` runs. That was already true before this commit, which registered no handler until then, so it isn't a regression. It also falls outside the authorized web-page scope.\n\nI agree with the gap you listed: none of this was run in a real macOS app."
